2186484055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484184 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484184 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484184 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484184 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484184 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e38aa8a0-730c-5f61-9672-fbfd36ec5e9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t-3, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01982d7c-8631-5513-adcd-2dc3bb87a98b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484194 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+(t-3)^{2}+(t)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-6t+9)+{t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =9 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-6t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=(0)-3=-3 \\\\ z=(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-3,0).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(2)=2 \\\\ y=(2)-3=-1 \\\\ z=(2)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(2,-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-3,0)$ and $(2,-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x-2=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03ac4047-3b67-5e2f-8026-813b5d163c02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484195 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx-2=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=3t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2(t+2)^{2}+4(t-1)^{2} & =(3t)^{2} \\\\ 2({t}^{2}+4t+4)+4({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =9{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+8t+8+4{t}^{2}-8t+4 & =9{t}^{2} \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 3(t+2)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2+2=0 \\\\ y=-2-1=-3 \\\\ z=3(-2)=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-3,-6).$\n\n- For $t=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2=4 \\\\ y=2-1=1 \\\\ z=3(2)=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,1,6).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-3,-6)$ and $(4,1,6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x+1=y-1=\\frac{z}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04b22284-5add-5373-b896-4f7022795490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484196 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx+1=y-1=\\frac{z}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t-1 \\\\ y=t+1 \\\\ z=2t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2(t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-2t+1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-4t+2+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2t-3 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3-1=-4 \\\\ y=-3+1=-2 \\\\ z=2(-3)=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-4,-2,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(0,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-4,-2,-6)$ and $(0,2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1071 "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f2db1db-7bfe-5b3b-ab6d-846d64109b8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484215 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(1-y)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y-1)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(1,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=100$ and the line $\\frac{x-5}{2}=-y=z+6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54e46f90-207e-55ef-98ea-cffcdc18e2c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484223 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5 \\\\ y=-t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y=-(0)=0 \\\\ z=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-\\frac{4}{3})+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y=-(-\\frac{4}{3})=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z=(-\\frac{4}{3})-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2(z-2)^{2}$ and the plane $2z-x-10=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58fab8e8-c40a-5f0c-89d7-e35fb797a4f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484226 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-x-10=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}+5\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+5-2)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3x+9) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+18 \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-6x+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)-6+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{(x-2)}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{(x-2)}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{24} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $6z-6={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ and the plane $x+y=2$ onto $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75d5fcc2-1404-5925-8c53-e5c58b19a741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484233 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=2t+2, \\\\ z=t+6\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7819f027-d28d-5660-b11c-cd0f95f720fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z-4=(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$ and the plane $x+y=-4$ onto $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e565e12-d691-5857-8d63-02efd0af7105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484241 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=-4\\,⟹\\,x=-4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-4 & =((-4-y)-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =(-y-6)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & ={y}^{2}+12y+36+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+12x+36 \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}({y}^{2}+8y+24) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(({y}^{2}+8y+16)+8) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+12 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=2t, \\\\ z=-2t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83fca566-7995-55f0-a5c4-c64563aea218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z=-2(-\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z=-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+7)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=81$ and the line $-\\frac{x}{2}=2-y=\\frac{z+1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "925fed75-7149-58ca-b2ab-03e5b43ab0a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484250 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{2-y}{1}=\\frac{z+1}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t \\\\ y=2-t \\\\ z=2t-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (-2t)^{2}+((2-t)+7)^{2}+((2t-1)+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (-2t)^{2}+(9-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2} & =81 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+(81-18t+{t}^{2})+4{t}^{2} & =81 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}-18t+81 & =81 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}-18t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2(0)=0 \\\\ y=2-(0)=2 \\\\ z=2(0)-1=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,2,-1).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2(2)=-4 \\\\ y=2-(2)=0 \\\\ z=2(2)-1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-4,0,3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,2,-1)$ and $(-4,0,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=2-t, \\\\ z=t-1\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf7120f6-4fae-5d19-b842-ba88886669a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+4(2-t)^{2}+2(t-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4(4-4t+{t}^{2})+2({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+16-16t+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}-4t+2 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+18 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+10 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=1.$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)=2 \\\\ y=2-1=1 \\\\ z=1-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=10$ and the plane $z-y=2$ onto the $xy$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3934760-11ef-5a3e-86ba-585bb0d00514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484293 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
